Sakura is a lightweight TUI display manager built exclusively for FreeBSD. It
runs on the vt(4) console rather than under a graphical server, authenticates
through OpenPAM, and launches X11, Wayland or plain console sessions.

Because it targets one platform, it talks to it directly: shutdown and reboot go
through reboot(2), virtual-terminal switching and the keyboard LEDs through
consio and kbio ioctls, and battery status through a sysctl MIB. None of that
shells out to an external helper, and no session bus or login-manager framework
is involved. The one program installed beside the binary is sakura_wrapper,
which /etc/gettytab names so getty(8) can start Sakura.

Its distinguishing feature is an animated GIF wallpaper rendered onto the text
console using quadrant and shade glyphs, which every stock FreeBSD console font
already provides. Seven other backgrounds ship with it, including a Lua-scripted
one, and the login box, colours, corner widgets and key bindings are all
configurable.

Sakura is the login layer of the Sakura desktop, alongside the hikari-sakura
compositor and the Sofi shell, but depends on neither and will launch any
session another display manager would.
